 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "You want a koi pond like the ones in Japan, so you dig a hole and fill it with a hose.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Next morning, half the water is gone.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Your soil is sandy, and the water just soaks down into the ground.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you line the hole with a thick rubber liner. Now it holds every drop.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then you buy koi from Niigata, where farmers have bred them for about two hundred years.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But two weeks later, the water is green and smells like a swamp.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Fish poop turns into ammonia, which is toxic, and the sun feeds the algae.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you add a pump that pulls water from the bottom of the pond.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It runs through a filter full of bacteria that turn ammonia into much safer nitrate.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then it falls back in as a little waterfall, adding oxygen for the fish.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "You plant reeds around the edge to shade the water and soak up the nitrate.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Koi keepers also go deep, so the water stays cool in summer and never freezes solid.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now the water is clear enough to count every fish.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just built a real koi pond. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
